{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"FIXED AND VERIFIED — the repair rode story b4e3's commit, not a commit of its own.\n\nWHERE THE FIX LIVES: commit 36e134401 (\"b4e3: mint DC inbound identities as jira family\"), on\norigin/main and reachable from this worktree's HEAD (verified with `git merge-base --is-ancestor`).\nIts message says so explicitly: \"Folds in bug 5f48. Two defects that compound, so they land\ntogether.\" Its trailer names `rebar-ticket: b4e3-1de0-3188-40a1` only, which is why this close\nneeds --force-close: no commit carries a 5f48 trailer, exactly as with 2bc7 earlier in this epic.\n\nBOTH HALVES LANDED — the complete fix this ticket's correction comment demanded:\n 1. THE ACTIVE GAP (guard) — `_ensure_inbound_assignee_identity` (apply_inbound_records.py:105) now\n    reads `for key in (\"accountId\", \"name\")`, Cloud's key FIRST so Cloud behaviour is unchanged,\n    falling back to DC's `name`. Previously it returned at an accountId-only guard, so DC minted\n    nothing and nothing raised.\n 2. THE LATENT LANDMINE (channel) — `_identity_provider_for` asks the BACKEND for its\n    `identity_family` rather than using the per-deployment `vendor`, so the mint validates against\n    the per-family vocabulary. The commit records why the family is DECLARED rather than derived:\n    splitting the vendor on \"-\" fails OPEN (a future `import-foo` backend would collapse onto\n    `import`, a real channel, and mis-stamp provenance silently), while a lookup table in the core\n    reintroduces vendor literals that test_backend_neutrality forbids.\n\nACCEPTANCE CRITERIA, each mapped to a test in\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_identity_dc_inbound_heldout.py` — **9 passed in 5.78s** locally:\n * \"an inbound apply under the DC backend mints a ghost identity successfully, asserted by a test\n   that FAILS on today's code\" -> `test_a_dc_shaped_assignee_mints_a_ghost_identity`.\n * \"minted under the shared 'jira' provider/channel, matching the ticket path\" ->\n   `test_the_dc_identity_is_minted_under_the_SHARED_jira_provider`, plus\n   `test_both_jira_backends_declare_the_shared_identity_family` pinning the declaration the design\n   rests on.\n * \"a test asserts the failure is not merely swallowed — the mint is observed to SUCCEED\" ->\n   `test_the_mint_is_observed_to_SUCCEED_not_merely_to_not_raise`. This is the criterion that\n   matters most here, because the call site swallows every exception; \"nothing raised\" was true\n   while nothing worked.\n * \"CREATION_CHANNELS is UNCHANGED (no vocabulary fork, no migration)\" ->\n   `test_creation_channels_vocabulary_is_unchanged`; confirmed directly in source —\n   `src/rebar/reducer/_version.py:160` still reads\n   `frozenset({\"cli\", \"mcp\", \"python\", \"jira\", \"import\", \"unknown\"})`.\n\nBEYOND THE STATED ACs, two tests guard the failure modes this ticket warned about:\n * `test_fixing_only_the_guard_would_swallow_a_channel_rejection` pins the HALF-FIXED state — it\n   simulates accepting DC's name while leaving the vendor as the channel and asserts the swallowed\n   exception's cause. That is what stops the two halves being split apart later, which was the\n   correction comment's central worry: the obvious one-line fix to the guard alone turns a silent\n   no-op into a swallowed exception, strictly harder to diagnose.\n * `test_cloud_accountid_path_is_unchanged` and\n   `test_an_assignee_with_neither_identifier_still_mints_nothing` pin that the guard NARROWED\n   rather than vanished.\n\nWHY THIS GATES J11: recorded on [rebar:5200] as `depends_on`. J11 exists to prove data actually\nmoves; an inbound identity failure that converges and reports success is precisely the defect class\nthat would let J11 report success over broken behaviour. That dependency is now satisfied.\n\nCLOSE CLASS: plan_defect. The DC inbound path was designed around Cloud's identifier and derived the\ncreation channel from the per-deployment vendor; neither was a regression and neither predated this\nepic's work — the design itself did not account for a second deployment shape."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785532138997766001,"uuid":"9c9305c8-60e2-4a9c-aa7a-9bc045cf7e44"}